Problem statement (under 100 words):
Qatar imports roughly 60–75% of its frozen whole chicken from Brazil via weeks-long ocean freight, arriving under a well-maintained cold chain. The real risk begins after import: Qatar's own municipal enforcement records show a decade of recurring violations — restaurants thawing and re-freezing frozen chicken, warehouses forging expiry dates, and tonnes of rotten or tampered frozen meat seized. These breaches happen during informal, manual domestic handling (ports, warehouses, distributors, retailers) where no affordable, tamper-evident monitoring exists, and are currently only caught through random physical raids, after the product may have already reached consumers.
Solution (under 100 words):
A packaging strip with food-based color indicator squares (biodegradable, non-toxic pigments like anthocyanins) plus a camera-calibration square, embedded in frozen chicken packaging. One square physically detects any thaw event instantly; others reveal graded spoilage from bacterial byproducts if thawing persisted. At each supply chain custody change (port, cold storage, distributor, retailer), a worker scans the strip via an offline-capable app or SMS/USSD, logging location, timestamp, and stage alongside the color reading. A BDI agent reasons over this data to detect anomalies and trigger actions — alerts, rerouting, or shipment holds — creating an auditable, low-cost trail across Qatar's domestic cold chain.
